.TH WAMPES.CONF 5 "14 August 2026" Linux "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
wampes.conf \- WAMPES nodes reachable from this machine
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
The AX.25 stack left the Linux kernel in 7.1.  A
.B WAMPES
node has a complete one in user space and lends it to programs linked
against
.BR libax25 ,
over a socket the node offers for the purpose.  This file says which nodes
exist and where each of them listens.
.LP
It is read by
.B libax25
itself, once, when a program first needs a node.  Nothing else reads it and
the node never sees it.
.SH "FILE FORMAT"
.LP
Comments start with
.B #
and run to the end of the line.  Blank lines are ignored.  Everything else is
one node per line, fields separated by whitespace:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.I name address \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
.fi
.RE
.TP
.I name
What stands before the colon in an
.BR axports (5)
entry.  The entries
.I wampes:hfb
and
.I wampes:70cm
are two ports of the node
.IR wampes .
The name is resolved here, on this side, and is never sent to the node; what
follows the colon is, because it names one of the node's own AX.25 ports.
Case is ignored.
.TP
.I address
Where the node listens.  Either a path, beginning with
.BR / ,
for its unix socket \-\-
.I /usr/local/wampes/sockets/ax25
in a default installation \-\- or
.I host:port
for its TCP service.  An IPv6 address literal goes in brackets, as in
.IR [fd00::5]:8010 ,
because an address full of colons cannot otherwise be told from the port
that follows it.  A host name is resolved with
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
and may answer with either family.
.TP
.I description
Free text, ignored.
.SH "THE TCP SERVICE"
.LP
A node keeps its TCP service switched off unless its
.I net.rc
says
.BR "start axtcp" ,
and when it is on it listens on 127.0.0.1 and ::1 only.  A TCP port carries
no rights of its own: switching it on is the statement that every account on
that machine may use the transmitter.  Reaching a node over TCP from
elsewhere means saying the same about the network in between.
.LP
The unix socket is the other way round.  It carries its rights in its mode \-\-
0660 and group
.B hams
where that group exists \-\- so the sysop decides who may use it with the
tools the file system already has.
.SH "WITHOUT THIS FILE"
.LP
The file is optional.  Without it a single node at
.I /usr/local/wampes/sockets/ax25
is assumed, which is what a machine with one WAMPES on it wants and spares it
a configuration file that would only ever hold one line.
.LP
The environment variable
.B WAMPES_SOCKET
overrides both, for every node, and is meant for trying something out rather
than for running a station.
.SH EXAMPLE
.LP
.RS
.nf
# name          address                         description
wampes          /usr/local/wampes/sockets/ax25  the node on this machine
db0aaa          192.168.1.5:8010                the club node, over TCP
db0bbb          [fd00::5]:8010                  the same, over IPv6
.fi
.RE
.LP
and in
.BR axports (5),
one entry per port of a node, each with a callsign of its own:
.LP
.RS
.nf
# name          callsign  baud  paclen  window  description
wampes:hfb      DL9SAU-1  9600  256     2       node wampes, port hfb
wampes:70cm     DL9SAU-2  9600  256     2       node wampes, port 70cm
db0aaa:hf1      DL9SAU-3  1200  128     1       club node, HF
.fi
.RE
.LP
One entry per port and not per node, because
.BR axports (5)
refuses duplicate callsigns and every port of a WAMPES node has a callsign of
its own anyway.  That callsign is what an outgoing connection uses as its
source unless the program binds one itself \-\-
.B call \-s
does.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.LP
A line without an address, a name or address that is too long, or more nodes
than the built-in limit are reported on standard error and the line is
skipped; the rest of the file is still read.
.LP
A port name that no
.BR axports (5)
entry matches is refused before any socket is opened.  A port the node does
not have is refused by the node, and
.BR connect (2)
fails with ENODEV.
.SH FILES
.LP
/etc/ax25/wampes.conf
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR call (1),
.BR ax25 (4),
.BR axports (5),
.BR agwpe.conf (5).
